1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,655
Same AI, same question.

2
00:00:01,655 --> 00:00:04,161
This prompt gets a bland, generic answer.

3
00:00:04,161 --> 00:00:07,462
This one gets exactly what you need, on the first try.

4
00:00:07,462 --> 00:00:09,662
The difference isn't a secret trick.

5
00:00:09,662 --> 00:00:13,208
It's five simple techniques, and they work in any chatbot.

6
00:00:13,907 --> 00:00:21,738
In the next eight minutes, you'll learn five prompt techniques that come straight from the official guides by OpenAI, Anthropic and Google.

7
00:00:21,738 --> 00:00:25,174
They work with ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot, any of them.

8
00:00:25,174 --> 00:00:26,357
No experience needed.

9
00:00:26,357 --> 00:00:32,497
And around the middle, I'll show you the mistake almost everyone makes, that quietly makes the answers worse.

10
00:00:33,198 --> 00:00:33,997
Here they are.

11
00:00:33,997 --> 00:00:35,197
One, give it context.

12
00:00:35,197 --> 00:00:37,255
Two, say exactly what you want back.

13
00:00:37,255 --> 00:00:38,569
Three, show an example.

14
00:00:38,569 --> 00:00:40,454
Four, break big tasks into steps.

15
00:00:40,454 --> 00:00:42,683
And five, let the AI ask you questions.

16
00:00:42,683 --> 00:00:51,254
We'll use one real-life task the whole way through, a product description for a small online shop, so you can see the answer get better at every step.

17
00:00:51,954 --> 00:00:53,198
Technique one, context.

18
00:00:53,198 --> 00:00:56,445
The AI knows nothing about you, your business, or your goal.

19
00:00:56,445 --> 00:00:57,743
So here's a simple test.

20
00:00:57,743 --> 00:01:02,179
Imagine you're handing this task to a smart new coworker, on their very first day.

21
00:01:02,179 --> 00:01:03,748
What would they need to know?

22
00:01:03,748 --> 00:01:04,397
Who you are.

23
00:01:04,397 --> 00:01:05,750
What exactly the task is.

24
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:06,940
The important details.

25
00:01:06,940 --> 00:01:08,347
And who the result is for.

26
00:01:09,047 --> 00:01:12,878
So instead of, write a description for my candle, you write this.

27
00:01:12,878 --> 00:01:15,000
I sell handmade soy candles on Etsy.

28
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,006
Write a product description for my lavender candle.

29
00:01:18,006 --> 00:01:21,483
It burns for forty hours and comes in a reusable glass jar.

30
00:01:21,483 --> 00:01:23,192
Most buyers get it as a gift.

31
00:01:23,192 --> 00:01:27,082
Four short sentences, and each one answers one of those questions.

32
00:01:27,782 --> 00:01:30,277
And here's the kind of answer you get now.

33
00:01:30,277 --> 00:01:34,732
It mentions the forty hours, the reusable jar, and it talks to gift buyers.

34
00:01:34,732 --> 00:01:35,386
Quick note.

35
00:01:35,386 --> 00:01:38,000
The replies in this video are illustrations.

36
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,108
Real answers change every time you ask, but this is the kind of difference you'll see.

37
00:01:43,808 --> 00:01:44,334
Pro move.

38
00:01:44,334 --> 00:01:46,205
You can also give the AI a role.

39
00:01:46,205 --> 00:01:51,115
Start with something like, you are an experienced copywriter for small online shops.

40
00:01:51,115 --> 00:01:53,863
Google's guide for Gemini calls this a persona.

41
00:01:53,863 --> 00:02:00,819
It sets the vocabulary and the level of detail, so the answer sounds like it came from someone who does this every day.

42
00:02:00,819 --> 00:02:03,742
Just keep it realistic, and still add the context.

43
00:02:04,442 --> 00:02:05,279
Technique two.

44
00:02:05,279 --> 00:02:07,132
Say exactly what you want back.

45
00:02:07,132 --> 00:02:11,077
If you don't, the AI guesses the length, the tone, and the layout.

46
00:02:11,077 --> 00:02:11,734
So tell it.

47
00:02:11,734 --> 00:02:12,870
Under eighty words.

48
00:02:12,870 --> 00:02:13,826
Warm, calm tone.

49
00:02:13,826 --> 00:02:14,902
And the structure.

50
00:02:14,902 --> 00:02:18,488
A title line, two short sentences, then three bullet points.

51
00:02:19,188 --> 00:02:22,162
Now the answer has exactly the shape you asked for.

52
00:02:22,162 --> 00:02:26,185
You can paste it straight into your shop, without rewriting anything.

53
00:02:26,185 --> 00:02:31,549
And because you asked for bullets, it's easy to scan on a phone, where a lot of people shop.

54
00:02:32,249 --> 00:02:32,785
Pro move.

55
00:02:32,785 --> 00:02:36,839
When you're comparing things, ask for a table, and name the columns.

56
00:02:36,839 --> 00:02:42,859
For example, compare these three laptops in a table, with columns for price, battery life and weight.

57
00:02:42,859 --> 00:02:44,588
One look, and you can decide.

58
00:02:45,288 --> 00:02:47,558
Now, the mistake almost everyone makes.

59
00:02:47,558 --> 00:02:51,457
When the answer isn't right, we pile on rules about what not to do.

60
00:02:51,457 --> 00:02:52,388
Don't be boring.

61
00:02:52,388 --> 00:02:53,377
Don't use jargon.

62
00:02:53,377 --> 00:02:54,483
Don't make it long.

63
00:02:54,483 --> 00:02:57,741
The problem is, a don't only tells the AI what to avoid.

64
00:02:57,741 --> 00:02:59,953
It doesn't tell it what to do instead.

65
00:03:00,653 --> 00:03:03,001
OpenAI's own guide gives this exact advice.

66
00:03:03,001 --> 00:03:05,294
Say what to do, instead of what not to do.

67
00:03:05,294 --> 00:03:09,444
So, don't use jargon, becomes, use words a twelve year old would understand.

68
00:03:09,444 --> 00:03:12,339
Don't make it long, becomes, three sentences maximum.

69
00:03:12,339 --> 00:03:15,560
And don't be boring, becomes, start with a surprising fact.

70
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:17,308
Every rule now points somewhere.

71
00:03:17,308 --> 00:03:19,328
By the way, this works on people too.

72
00:03:20,028 --> 00:03:21,887
Technique three, show an example.

73
00:03:21,887 --> 00:03:23,915
Describing a style in words is hard.

74
00:03:23,915 --> 00:03:24,985
Showing it is easy.

75
00:03:24,985 --> 00:03:29,492
If you already have something you like, paste it in, and ask the AI to match it.

76
00:03:29,492 --> 00:03:35,632
This is called few-shot prompting, and it's one of the most reliable techniques in all three official guides.

77
00:03:36,332 --> 00:03:38,421
And the answer copies the rhythm.

78
00:03:38,421 --> 00:03:39,244
A short name.

79
00:03:39,244 --> 00:03:40,194
A playful line.

80
00:03:40,194 --> 00:03:41,397
Then the burn time.

81
00:03:41,397 --> 00:03:45,386
One good example often beats a whole paragraph of instructions.

82
00:03:46,086 --> 00:03:46,693
Quick tip.

83
00:03:46,693 --> 00:03:53,556
When you paste text into a prompt, put it between quotes, or between simple tags, like example in angle brackets.

84
00:03:53,556 --> 00:03:58,961
That way the AI knows exactly where your instructions end, and where the material begins.

85
00:03:58,961 --> 00:04:02,484
This matters most when you paste long emails or documents.

86
00:04:02,484 --> 00:04:04,974
Both OpenAI and Anthropic recommend this.

87
00:04:05,674 --> 00:04:06,475
Technique four.

88
00:04:06,475 --> 00:04:07,915
Break big tasks into steps.

89
00:04:07,915 --> 00:04:13,038
If you ask for everything in one giant prompt, you usually get an average result for every part.

90
00:04:13,038 --> 00:04:16,186
Instead, ask for one step, check it, then ask for the next.

91
00:04:16,186 --> 00:04:18,533
For our shop, step one, list ten name ideas.

92
00:04:18,533 --> 00:04:20,294
Step two, you pick your favorite.

93
00:04:20,294 --> 00:04:22,108
Step three, write the description.

94
00:04:22,108 --> 00:04:24,563
Step four, write three titles for the listing.

95
00:04:25,263 --> 00:04:26,275
It looks like this.

96
00:04:26,275 --> 00:04:30,273
Give me ten name ideas for a lavender soy candle, just the names, numbered.

97
00:04:30,273 --> 00:04:35,229
Then you pick one, and in your next message, you say, write the description for number three.

98
00:04:35,229 --> 00:04:39,280
The chat remembers the conversation, so you don't need to repeat everything.

99
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:43,383
And if one step goes wrong, you fix just that step, instead of starting over.

100
00:04:44,084 --> 00:04:47,130
And for anything with logic or math, add one line.

101
00:04:47,130 --> 00:04:50,602
Work through it step by step, then give the final answer.

102
00:04:50,602 --> 00:04:56,572
The official guides call this giving the model time to think, and it helps it make fewer mistakes.

103
00:04:57,272 --> 00:05:00,784
Technique five, my favorite, and the one almost nobody uses.

104
00:05:00,784 --> 00:05:04,412
Instead of guessing what to include, let the AI interview you.

105
00:05:04,412 --> 00:05:06,460
Just end your prompt with one line.

106
00:05:06,460 --> 00:05:11,084
Before you start, ask me up to three questions about anything you need to know.

107
00:05:11,783 --> 00:05:13,778
Now the AI asks for what's missing.

108
00:05:13,778 --> 00:05:14,746
What's the price?

109
00:05:14,746 --> 00:05:15,715
Is the wax vegan?

110
00:05:15,715 --> 00:05:17,310
Where will this text appear?

111
00:05:17,310 --> 00:05:22,438
You answer in a few words, and the result fits your situation, instead of a generic guess.

112
00:05:22,438 --> 00:05:26,597
It also shows you what you forgot to mention, which is useful on its own.

113
00:05:27,298 --> 00:05:30,528
And remember, the first answer is a draft, not the final.

114
00:05:30,528 --> 00:05:32,285
Don't just say, make it better.

115
00:05:32,285 --> 00:05:35,176
Give specific feedback, like you would to a person.

116
00:05:35,176 --> 00:05:36,536
Keep the first sentence.

117
00:05:36,536 --> 00:05:37,953
Make the bullets shorter.

118
00:05:37,953 --> 00:05:40,277
Swap the word cozy for something fresher.

119
00:05:40,977 --> 00:05:41,317
Bonus.

120
00:05:41,317 --> 00:05:46,597
Put all five together, and you get a template you can save in a note, and reuse for anything.

121
00:05:46,597 --> 00:05:47,051
Context.

122
00:05:47,051 --> 00:05:47,561
The task.

123
00:05:47,561 --> 00:05:48,186
The format.

124
00:05:48,186 --> 00:05:48,810
An example.

125
00:05:48,810 --> 00:05:50,513
And the magic line at the end.

126
00:05:50,513 --> 00:05:52,557
Fill in the blanks, and you're done.

127
00:05:52,557 --> 00:05:56,473
I keep mine in a notes app, and paste it whenever I start a new chat.

128
00:05:57,173 --> 00:06:00,360
Let's try the template on something completely different.

129
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:01,925
Say you want to learn Excel.

130
00:06:01,925 --> 00:06:04,552
I'm a total beginner with thirty minutes a day.

131
00:06:04,552 --> 00:06:07,012
Create a two week study plan to learn Excel.

132
00:06:07,012 --> 00:06:10,925
Format, a table with three columns, day, topic, and one practice task.

133
00:06:10,925 --> 00:06:11,987
And the magic line.

134
00:06:11,987 --> 00:06:14,615
Before you start, ask me up to three questions.

135
00:06:14,615 --> 00:06:16,404
Same five parts, brand new task.

136
00:06:17,103 --> 00:06:21,574
The AI asks what version of Excel you have, and what you want to use it for.

137
00:06:21,574 --> 00:06:26,162
You answer, and you get a plan that fits your week, in a table you can follow.

138
00:06:26,162 --> 00:06:27,220
Same AI as before.

139
00:06:27,220 --> 00:06:28,632
The prompt did the work.

140
00:06:29,332 --> 00:06:31,084
Four quick mistakes to avoid.

141
00:06:31,084 --> 00:06:32,715
First, trusting every fact.

142
00:06:32,715 --> 00:06:38,635
AI can sound completely sure and still be wrong, so always check numbers, dates, names and quotes.

143
00:06:38,635 --> 00:06:40,809
Second, pasting private information.

144
00:06:40,809 --> 00:06:44,313
Never paste passwords, bank details, or confidential work.

145
00:06:44,313 --> 00:06:46,669
Third, asking ten things in one prompt.

146
00:06:46,669 --> 00:06:47,937
One step per message.

147
00:06:47,937 --> 00:06:50,474
And fourth, giving up after the first try.

148
00:06:50,474 --> 00:06:52,286
The magic is in the follow-up.

149
00:06:52,286 --> 00:06:58,689
And when the answer really matters, ask where the information comes from, then check that source yourself.

150
00:06:59,389 --> 00:06:59,829
Pro tip.

151
00:06:59,829 --> 00:07:03,348
In most chat apps, pressing Enter sends your message right away.

152
00:07:03,348 --> 00:07:06,811
Press Shift and Enter together instead, and you get a new line.

153
00:07:06,811 --> 00:07:13,627
That's how you write a clean prompt, with context, format and example on separate lines, without sending it halfway through.

154
00:07:14,327 --> 00:07:15,052
Quick recap.

155
00:07:15,052 --> 00:07:17,708
Give it context, with the new coworker test.

156
00:07:17,708 --> 00:07:19,579
Say exactly what you want back.

157
00:07:19,579 --> 00:07:20,545
Show an example.

158
00:07:20,545 --> 00:07:22,175
Break big tasks into steps.

159
00:07:22,175 --> 00:07:24,167
And let the AI ask you questions.

160
00:07:24,167 --> 00:07:26,582
Plus, say what to do, not what to avoid.

161
00:07:26,582 --> 00:07:29,781
Five techniques, and better answers on the first try.

162
